1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,513
Every backlog drifts.

2
00:00:01,533 --> 00:00:04,412
Stories without acceptance criteria.

3
00:00:04,432 --> 00:00:06,700
Features without a parent.

4
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:08,271
Sizes nobody agreed.

5
00:00:08,291 --> 00:00:11,170
Halyard is the product owner's mate.

6
00:00:11,190 --> 00:00:14,920
It reads the backlog, finds the drift,
drafts the fix,

7
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:17,490
and waits for a person to approve it.

8
00:00:17,510 --> 00:00:19,404
Nothing moves without your say.

9
00:00:19,954 --> 00:00:22,277
Here is how Halyard fits together.

10
00:00:22,297 --> 00:00:24,502
Everything runs on one machine.

11
00:00:24,522 --> 00:00:27,378
A PowerShell launcher compiles the engine
in memory,

12
00:00:27,398 --> 00:00:29,666
and the browser talks to it on localhost.

13
00:00:30,366 --> 00:00:35,832
Inside the engine: review,
intelligence for drafting and sizing,

14
00:00:35,852 --> 00:00:38,490
planning, and a proposal store.

15
00:00:38,510 --> 00:00:40,228
Every change starts as a proposal.

16
00:00:40,928 --> 00:00:44,048
Halyard reads your backlog from GitHub,
read only.

17
00:00:44,068 --> 00:00:49,162
The write gateway lets it write to one
place: the sandbox backlog you configure.

18
00:00:49,862 --> 00:00:52,418
Model calls go through Fairlead and
nowhere else.

19
00:00:52,438 --> 00:00:56,658
Halyard's own key sits in Windows
Credential Manager.

20
00:00:56,678 --> 00:00:59,823
The OpenAI key stays in Lazarette,
shared to Fairlead.

21
00:01:00,523 --> 00:01:03,299
Out the other side:
four tables for Semaphore,

22
00:01:03,319 --> 00:01:06,055
and the weekly dashboard as an Outlook
draft.

23
00:01:06,755 --> 00:01:09,527
Dashed means planned:
live Shellback skills,

24
00:01:09,547 --> 00:01:11,705
and a runner on your repositories.

25
00:01:12,405 --> 00:01:13,651
One rule holds it together.

26
00:01:14,466 --> 00:01:16,665
Nothing is written until a person
approves.

27
00:01:17,415 --> 00:01:20,484
Every tool in the fleet needs a model key.

28
00:01:20,504 --> 00:01:23,095
At OpenAI, create a new secret key.

29
00:01:23,853 --> 00:01:26,091
Name it something generic.

30
00:01:26,111 --> 00:01:27,957
I call mine fleet-tools.

31
00:01:27,977 --> 00:01:29,935
Seven days is plenty.

32
00:01:30,759 --> 00:01:31,759
Copy it once.

33
00:01:32,316 --> 00:01:33,863
OpenAI never shows it again.

34
00:01:33,883 --> 00:01:37,832
The key goes into Lazarette,
the encrypted vault on this machine.

35
00:01:38,532 --> 00:01:41,440
Test credential checks it against OpenAI's
read-only endpoint.

36
00:01:42,182 --> 00:01:43,192
HTTP two hundred.

37
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:44,860
The key is good.

38
00:01:45,134 --> 00:01:49,889
Share it with Fairlead by name,
and Fairlead sees only what you share.

39
00:01:50,589 --> 00:01:53,251
Fairlead reads it from the vault and
connects.

40
00:01:53,271 --> 00:01:55,382
Fifty-three models, in about a second.

41
00:01:55,984 --> 00:01:58,038
Fairlead gives each tool its own key.

42
00:01:58,738 --> 00:02:00,114
Pick Halyard and install it.

43
00:02:00,814 --> 00:02:02,817
It lands in Windows Credential Manager.

44
00:02:03,517 --> 00:02:08,069
Halyard's config file names the backlog it
reads, the backlog it may write to,

45
00:02:08,089 --> 00:02:09,182
and the write mode.

46
00:02:09,202 --> 00:02:11,649
The browser can't change any of it.

47
00:02:12,349 --> 00:02:13,849
Test the model.

48
00:02:13,869 --> 00:02:16,262
Under two seconds, end to end.

49
00:02:17,012 --> 00:02:20,811
For this walkthrough we seed a synthetic
backlog.

50
00:02:20,831 --> 00:02:24,201
Thirty items,
a quarter of them with deliberate gaps.

51
00:02:24,901 --> 00:02:27,622
Each one is a real GitHub issue with a run
label,

52
00:02:27,642 --> 00:02:30,195
so the whole run comes out again in one
click.

53
00:02:30,215 --> 00:02:33,822
It takes about two minutes,
paced for the rate limits.

54
00:02:34,522 --> 00:02:36,470
Thirty of thirty.

55
00:02:36,490 --> 00:02:38,681
The backlog is live.

56
00:02:39,061 --> 00:02:43,552
The overview shows the shape of the
backlog and what needs attention:

57
00:02:43,572 --> 00:02:46,821
items to review, missing parents,
topology errors.

58
00:02:46,841 --> 00:02:51,451
Scroll down for the findings by severity,
and the next actions.

59
00:02:52,151 --> 00:02:55,807
Review checks every item against its
level's definition of ready and the parent

60
00:02:55,827 --> 00:02:56,827
chain.

61
00:02:56,847 --> 00:02:59,033
Each finding names the fix that closes it.

62
00:02:59,733 --> 00:03:02,640
Hierarchy shows the tree, theme to task.

63
00:03:02,890 --> 00:03:06,890
Draft missing sections sends each gap to
the model through Fairlead.

64
00:03:06,910 --> 00:03:09,383
Real model, a few seconds per item.

65
00:03:10,083 --> 00:03:12,030
Six drafts, all high confidence.

66
00:03:12,050 --> 00:03:14,429
Each is a proposal with a reason.

67
00:03:14,449 --> 00:03:16,273
Open one and read the diff:

68
00:03:16,293 --> 00:03:20,099
the model adds what's missing and leaves
the rest alone.

69
00:03:20,119 --> 00:03:24,487
And if it ever drops a section,
Halyard puts it back word for word.

70
00:03:25,187 --> 00:03:28,674
Approve the high-confidence drafts,
then apply.

71
00:03:28,694 --> 00:03:32,507
Six written to the backlog on GitHub,
and the apply is recorded.

72
00:03:32,527 --> 00:03:35,551
Every apply is recorded with what it
replaced.

73
00:03:36,107 --> 00:03:39,048
Undo previews first,
then restores the earlier text.

74
00:03:39,369 --> 00:03:40,499
Six items restored.

75
00:03:41,199 --> 00:03:44,285
Anything a person edited since is left
alone.

76
00:03:44,305 --> 00:03:46,390
The proposals go back to pending,

77
00:03:46,410 --> 00:03:49,280
so approve and apply again when you're
ready.

78
00:03:49,530 --> 00:03:51,425
Work comes in from anywhere.

79
00:03:51,445 --> 00:03:55,347
A raw ticket,
or a note typed straight into Intake.

80
00:03:55,367 --> 00:03:57,681
Two loose notes, no structure at all.

81
00:03:58,293 --> 00:03:59,293
Preview the drafts.

82
00:03:59,313 --> 00:04:04,081
Halyard turns each note into a structured
work item and copies it into the backlog,

83
00:04:04,101 --> 00:04:06,292
with a link back to where it started.

84
00:04:06,671 --> 00:04:09,000
Plan fills the next sprints from ready
stories.

85
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:11,498
Capacity comes from the team file.

86
00:04:11,518 --> 00:04:13,456
Owners are matched on skills,

87
00:04:13,476 --> 00:04:16,786
and anything that slipped last sprint goes
first.

88
00:04:17,486 --> 00:04:19,827
Skills come from Shellback peer cards
today.

89
00:04:20,513 --> 00:04:22,134
A live Shellback link is planned.

90
00:04:22,634 --> 00:04:26,088
Upward, Halyard drafts the portfolio story
for leadership.

91
00:04:26,108 --> 00:04:28,277
This one is the live model, thinking.

92
00:04:28,977 --> 00:04:32,114
Themes, priorities,
risks and the next quarter,

93
00:04:32,134 --> 00:04:34,532
with the facts every claim rests on.

94
00:04:34,552 --> 00:04:38,148
Everything it says about the backlog,
it can point to.

95
00:04:38,890 --> 00:04:44,869
The leadership page puts features on
track, velocity, ready backlog,

96
00:04:44,889 --> 00:04:48,044
capacity and blockers on one screen.

97
00:04:48,064 --> 00:04:51,908
One click exports backlog health for
Semaphore, as four tables ready to chart.

98
00:04:52,504 --> 00:04:55,145
The weekly dashboard opens in Outlook as a
draft,

99
00:04:55,165 --> 00:04:57,337
so a person reviews it and presses send.

100
00:04:58,022 --> 00:05:02,253
Next on the chart:
a runner on the team's repositories.

101
00:05:02,273 --> 00:05:06,882
It watches commits and pull requests,
and writes the story for the work it sees.

102
00:05:07,380 --> 00:05:09,343
Developers stop doing the admin.

103
00:05:09,363 --> 00:05:13,638
The product owner approves what the runner
proposes,

104
00:05:13,658 --> 00:05:16,941
through the same diff and the same gate.

105
00:05:16,961 --> 00:05:17,961
Production stays shut.

106
00:05:17,981 --> 00:05:21,350
Promote needs a production write mode,
an explicit allow flag,

107
00:05:21,370 --> 00:05:22,805
a typed phrase and a diff,

108
00:05:22,825 --> 00:05:25,680
and none of it can be switched on from the
browser.

109
00:05:26,380 --> 00:05:29,486
The write audit lists every write Halyard
made.

110
00:05:30,013 --> 00:05:31,836
Every one went to the backlog.

111
00:05:31,856 --> 00:05:33,189
Halyard proposes.

112
00:05:33,209 --> 00:05:34,304
People decide.

113
00:05:34,324 --> 00:05:35,657
That's the watch.

114
00:05:35,677 --> 00:05:40,274
This demo took longer to make than the
tool took to build.

115
00:05:40,294 --> 00:05:44,494
So why would you pay for Azure DevOps
Boards or Jira?

116
00:05:44,514 --> 00:05:46,351
The tools are changing,

117
00:05:46,371 --> 00:05:50,246
and compute and creativity are the new
frontier.
